CA,TCCA) from extracellular region to cytosol\", \"Transport (influx) of glycocholate and taurocholate by OATP-C\"], \"releaseDate\": \"2026-06-17\", \"schemaClass\": \"Reaction\", \"speciesName\": \"Mus musculus\", \"stId\": \"R-MMU-194083\", \"stIdVersion\": \"R-MMU-194083.1\"}], \"output\": [{\"className\": \"Set\", \"dbId\": 194097, \"displayName\": \"GCCA, TCCA [cytosol]\", \"inDisease\": false, \"maxDepth\": 2, \"name\": [\"GCCA, TCCA\", \"cholate bile salts\", \"glycocholate; taurocholate\"], \"producedByEvent\": [194083], \"schemaClass\": \"DefinedSet\", \"stId\": \"R-ALL-194097\", \"stIdVersion\": \"R-ALL-194097.1\"}], \"precedingEvent\": [{\"category\": \"transition\", \"className\": \"Reaction\", \"dbId\": 194153, \"displayName\": \"ABCC3 transports bile salts from cytosol to extracellular region\", \"followingEvent\": [194083], \"isInDisease\": false, \"isInferred\": false, \"maxDepth\": 1, \"name\": [\"ABCC3 transports bile salts from cytosol to extracellular region\", \"Efflux of bile salts from enterocytes to portal system by MRP3\"], \"releaseDate\": \"2007-05-15\", \"schemaClass\": \"Reaction\", \"speciesName\": \"Homo sapiens\", \"stId\": \"R-HSA-194153\", \"stIdVersion\": \"R-HSA-194153.3\"}], \"releaseDate\": \"2007-05-15\", \"reviewStatus\": {\"className\": \"ReviewStatus\", \"dbId\": 9821382, \"definition\": \"externally reviewed\", \"displayName\": \"five stars\", \"name\": [\"five stars\"], \"schemaClass\": \"ReviewStatus\"}, \"schemaClass\": \"Reaction\", \"species\": [{\"abbreviation\": \"HSA\", \"className\": \"Species\", \"dbId\": 48887, \"displayName\": \"Homo sapiens\", \"name\": [\"Homo sapiens\", \"H. sapiens\", \"Hs\", \"human\", \"man\"], \"schemaClass\": \"Species\", \"taxId\": \"9606\"}], \"speciesName\": \"Homo sapiens\", \"stId\": \"R-HSA-194083\", \"stIdVersion\": \"R-HSA-194083.5\", \"summation\": [{\"className\": \"Summation\", \"dbId\": 194095, \"displayName\": \"A molecule of extracellular glycocholate (GCCA) or taurochol...\", \"schemaClass\": \"Summation\", \"text\": \"A molecule of extracellular glycocholate (GCCA) or taurocholate (TCCA) is transported into the cytosol, mediated by OATP-C (SLCO1B1) in the plasma membrane. GCCA and TCCA exist in the blood as complexes with serum albumin (ALB), and its uptake by OATP-C must involve disruption of this complex, but the molecular mechanism coupling disruption and uptake is unknown. In the body, OATP-C is expressed on the basolateral surfaces of hepatocytes and may play a role in the uptake of GCCA and TCCA by the liver under physiological conditions (Kullak-Ublick et al. 2004, Trauner & Boyer 2002).\"}]}" ;
