to Ac-CoA\", \"followingEvent\": [8876889], \"isInDisease\": false, \"isInferred\": false, \"maxDepth\": 1, \"name\": [\"ACLY tetramer transforms CIT to Ac-CoA\", \"ATP + citrate + CoA => acetyl-CoA + ADP + oxaloacetate + phosphate\", \"Generation of Cytoplasmic Acetyl CoA from Citrate\"], \"releaseDate\": \"2004-07-06\", \"schemaClass\": \"Reaction\", \"speciesName\": \"Homo sapiens\", \"stId\": \"R-HSA-75848\", \"stIdVersion\": \"R-HSA-75848.7\"}, {\"category\": \"omitted\", \"className\": \"Reaction\", \"dbId\": 9856605, \"displayName\": \"Transcriptional activation of ACACB by ChREBP:MLX\", \"followingEvent\": [8876889], \"isInDisease\": false, \"isInferred\": false, \"maxDepth\": 1, \"name\": [\"Transcriptional activation of ACACB by ChREBP:MLX\"], \"releaseDate\": \"2024-03-27\", \"schemaClass\": \"BlackBoxEvent\", \"speciesName\": \"Homo sapiens\", \"stId\": \"R-HSA-9856605\", \"stIdVersion\": \"R-HSA-9856605.2\"}], \"previousReviewStatus\": {\"className\": \"ReviewStatus\", \"dbId\": 9821385, \"definition\": \"structure upated after externally reviewed and then internally reviewed\", \"displayName\": \"four stars\", \"name\": [\"four stars\"], \"schemaClass\": \"ReviewStatus\"}, \"regulatedBy\": [{\"className\": \"PositiveRegulation\", \"dbId\": 8876897, \"displayName\": \"Positive regulation by 'CIT [cytosol]'\", \"regulatedEntity\": [8876889], \"schemaClass\": \"PositiveRegulation\", \"stId\": \"R-HSA-8876897\", \"stIdVersion\": \"R-HSA-8876897.1\"}, {\"className\": \"PositiveRegulation\", \"dbId\": 8878972, \"displayName\": \"Positive regulation by 'MID1IP1:THRSP [cytosol]'\", \"regulatedEntity\": [8876889], \"schemaClass\": \"PositiveRegulation\", \"stId\": \"R-HSA-8878972\", \"stIdVersion\": \"R-HSA-8878972.1\"}], \"regulationReference\": [{\"className\": \"RegulationReference\", \"dbId\": 9641816, \"displayName\": \"Positive regulation by 'MID1IP1:THRSP [cytosol]' Hepatic expression of the SPOT 14 (S14) paralog S14-related (Mid1 interacting protein) is regulated by dietary carbohydrate\", \"schemaClass\": \"RegulationReference\"}], \"releaseDate\": \"2016-09-19\", \"reviewStatus\": {\"className\": \"ReviewStatus\", \"dbId\": 9821382, \"definition\": \"externally reviewed\", \"displayName\": \"five stars\", \"name\": [\"five stars\"], \"schemaClass\": \"ReviewStatus\"}, \"schemaClass\": \"Reaction\", \"species\": [{\"abbreviation\": \"HSA\", \"className\": \"Species\", \"dbId\": 48887, \"displayName\": \"Homo sapiens\", \"name\": [\"Homo sapiens\", \"H. sapiens\", \"Hs\", \"human\", \"man\"], \"schemaClass\": \"Species\", \"taxId\": \"9606\"}], \"speciesName\": \"Homo sapiens\", \"stId\": \"R-HSA-8876889\", \"stIdVersion\": \"R-HSA-8876889.5\", \"summation\": [{\"className\": \"Summation\", \"dbId\": 8876894, \"displayName\": \"Mitochondrial acetyl-CoA carboxylase 2 (ACACB, ACC2) (Kim et...\", \"schemaClass\": \"Summation\", \"text\": \"Mitochondrial acetyl-CoA carboxylase 2 (ACACB, ACC2) (Kim et al. 2007) catalyses the reaction of bicarbonate, ATP, and acetyl-CoA to form malonyl-CoA, ADP, and orthophosphate. The reaction is positively regulated by citrate. ACACB uses biotin (Btn) and two Mn2+ ions per subunit as cofactors and its activity is increased by polymerization (Kim et al. 2010, Ingaramo & Beckett 2012). ACACB is located on the outer mitochondrial membrane and is involved in the regulation of mitochondrial fatty acid oxidation through the inhibition of carnitine palmitoyltransferase 1 by its product malonyl-CoA (Abu-Elheiga et al. 2000).<br><br>Mid1-interacting protein 1 (MID1IP1, aka MIG12, SPOT14R, S14R) plays a role in the regulation of lipogenesis in the liver. It is rapidly upregulated by processes that induce lipogenesis (enhanced glucose metabolism, thyroid hormone administration) (Tsatsos et al. 2008). MID1IP1 forms a heterodimer with thyroid hormone-inducible hepatic protein (THRSP, aka SPOT14, S14), proposed to play the same role in lipogenesis as MID1IP1 (Aipoalani et al. 2010). This complex can polymerize acetyl-CoA carboxylases 1 and 2 (ACACA and B), the first committed enzymes in fatty acid (FA) synthesis. Polymerization enhances ACACA and ACACB enzyme activities (Kim et al. 2010).\"}]}" ;
