culus\", \"stId\": \"R-MMU-9020258\", \"stIdVersion\": \"R-MMU-9020258.1\"}], \"output\": [{\"className\": \"Set\", \"dbId\": 9024759, \"displayName\": \"RvD1, RvD2 [cytosol]\", \"inDisease\": false, \"maxDepth\": 2, \"name\": [\"RvD1, RvD2\"], \"producedByEvent\": [9020258], \"schemaClass\": \"DefinedSet\", \"stId\": \"R-ALL-9024759\", \"stIdVersion\": \"R-ALL-9024759.1\"}], \"precedingEvent\": [{\"category\": \"transition\", \"className\": \"Reaction\", \"dbId\": 9020255, \"displayName\": \"ALOX5 dehydrogenates 7(S)-Hp-17(S)-HDHA to 7S(8)-epoxy-17S-HDHA\", \"followingEvent\": [9020258], \"isChimeric\": false, \"isInDisease\": false, \"isInferred\": false, \"maxDepth\": 1, \"name\": [\"ALOX5 dehydrogenates 7(S)-Hp-17(S)-HDHA to 7S(8)-epoxy-17S-HDHA\"], \"releaseDate\": \"2018-03-21\", \"schemaClass\": \"Reaction\", \"speciesName\": \"Homo sapiens\", \"stId\": \"R-HSA-9020255\", \"stIdVersion\": \"R-HSA-9020255.2\"}], \"releaseDate\": \"2018-03-21\", \"reviewStatus\": {\"className\": \"ReviewStatus\", \"dbId\": 9821382, \"definition\": \"externally reviewed\", \"displayName\": \"five stars\", \"name\": [\"five stars\"], \"schemaClass\": \"ReviewStatus\"}, \"schemaClass\": \"Reaction\", \"species\": [{\"abbreviation\": \"HSA\", \"className\": \"Species\", \"dbId\": 48887, \"displayName\": \"Homo sapiens\", \"name\": [\"Homo sapiens\", \"H. sapiens\", \"Hs\", \"human\", \"man\"], \"schemaClass\": \"Species\", \"taxId\": \"9606\"}], \"speciesName\": \"Homo sapiens\", \"stId\": \"R-HSA-9020258\", \"stIdVersion\": \"R-HSA-9020258.2\", \"summation\": [{\"className\": \"Summation\", \"dbId\": 9024761, \"displayName\": \"Leukotriene A4 hydrolase (LTA4H) is a monomeric, soluble enz...\", \"schemaClass\": \"Summation\", \"text\": \"Leukotriene A4 hydrolase (LTA4H) is a monomeric, soluble enzyme that uses a Zn2+ cofactor to catalyse the hydrolysis of the allylic epoxide leukotriene A4 (LTA4) (McGee & Fitzpatrick 1985). LTA4H can also catalyse the hydrolysis of 7S(8)-epoxy-17(S)-hydroxydocosahexaenoic acid (7S(8)-epoxy-17(S)-HDHA) to the trihydroxydocosahexaenoic acids 7(S), 8(R), 17(S)-triHDHA and 7(S), 16(R), 17(S)-triHDHA (RvD1 and RvD2 respectively) (Sun et al. 2007, Spite et al. 2009, Serhan et al. 2002). The D-resolvins are anti-inflammatory, pro-resolving, and non-phlogistic (that is, they mediate the clearance of leukocytes without eliciting an inflammatory response) (Serhan et al. 2008).\"}]}" ;
